Equivalent topic, but a less provocative presentation

The top-level explanation is not that red possesses a uniquely powerful neurological property. There are several interacting mechanisms.

Feature contrast and visual pop-out. Research in visual search demonstrates that an object distinguished by a salient feature, such as colour, can be detected rapidly among competing objects. Crucially, salience depends on the surrounding environment. A red object is noticeable among grey objects; a grey object might be noticeable in a field of red objects.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\&sz=32)

PMC+1



Processing fluency. Large letters, clear typography, and a short message reduce the effort required to recognize the promise. A small thumbnail has very little time to communicate something meaningful before the viewer scrolls on.

Learned meaning. Red is associated with warnings, errors, urgency, prohibitions, and alerts in many visual contexts. Those associations can contribute to its effect, but color psychology is context-dependent. Research does not support a universal claim that red is always the most persuasive colour.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.annualreviews.org\&sz=32)

Annual Reviews



There is some experimental evidence from adjacent marketing. A 2014 field study examined more than 1.5 million web-ad impressions and found that red advertisements outperformed blue versions in one box-ad format, but the advantage was less pronounced for banner advertisements. That supports the possibility of a colour effect, not a universal rule about YouTube thumbnails.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.tandfonline.com\&sz=32)

Get Access



The strongest explanation is therefore:

The red draws attention to the words. The words communicate the opportunity or threat. And the implied payoff gives the viewer a reason to act.

Red is often the delivery mechanism, not the reason someone wants to click.

## 4. The most powerful research: the psychology of withheld information

This is the section I would build the centre of your video around.

### The information-gap theory of curiosity

In 1994, behavioural economist George Loewenstein published The Psychology of Curiosity: A Review and Reinterpretation.

His central proposal was that curiosity arises when someone becomes conscious of a gap between what they know and what they want to know.

In other words, you can create a desire for information by making someone aware of information they are missing.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://bibbase.org\&sz=32)

bibbase.org



Consider these three titles:

A

How YouTube Thumbnails Work

A direct description of the information available

B

Why Does This Thumbnail Work?

Introduces a specific question with an implied answer

C

The Secret YouTube Doesn't Want You to Know

Introduces mystery, hidden knowledge, and implied conflict

The second is a particularly elegant version because it can make someone curious about an object they otherwise wouldn't have thought about.

The thumbnail presents itself as a question requiring explanation.

Before seeing it, the viewer may not care about the psychology of thumbnails. After seeing it, they have a question.

The advertisement has potentially manufactured the very demand it offers to satisfy.

### But there's a catch: too much mystery can reduce clicks

A 2025 study published in Scientific Reports analysed 8,977 headline experiments, comparing different ways of describing the same article while controlling for the accompanying image.

It found that additional specificity sometimes increased CTR and sometimes reduced it.

The overall finding was consistent with an optimal level of information: too vague and the audience doesn't know why to care; too explicit and there may be little left to discover.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\&sz=32)

PubMed Central (PMC)



Conceptual illustration of the study's curvilinear finding, not measured values or a fitted curve.

This might be the most useful rule you take from the research:

A powerful title gives the viewer enough information to imagine a valuable answer, but not necessarily enough to remove their motivation to seek it.

Your question, Why does this thumbnail work?, follows that logic.

